lumina-lock (0.4.27) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Rebuilt the slide's sweeping highlight so that it keeps both things at once,
    which the previous two attempts each lost in turn. It is the same text twice
    now: the label at rest, and a full-white copy seen only through a window that
    travels across it. Two ordinary text items have no mask semantics to fight —
    both are drawn as text and keep their antialiasing, and the window only
    decides which part of the second one is shown.
  * What the earlier attempts ran into, for the record. With the text as a
    MultiEffect mask the sweep worked, but the glyph shape then comes out of the
    mask and the mask behaves as a hard threshold: the strokes were stepped at 8x
    beside ordinary labels that were smooth. Swapping source and mask let the
    same threshold swallow the band entirely — the label read as solid white, or
    vanished, depending on where the band was; the six-column profile showed
    either 255 everywhere or nothing at all. Measured across one sweep now: the
    resting columns hold at 180 and the band reaches 255 as it crosses.
  * The band's own edges are hard, which is what a band of light looks like. The
    glyphs inside it are not affected.

 -- Lumina <lumina@example.com>  Fri, 18 Sep 2026 14:05:00 +0800

lumina-lock (0.4.26) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Fixed the slide's label being drawn without antialiasing. It was built with
    the text as the mask and the gradient bar as the source, and the glyph shape
    then comes out of the mask: at 8x the strokes were hard-edged and had no
    subpixel colour, next to ordinary labels that had both. The text is the
    source now and the bar is the mask, so the glyphs are drawn as text, with
    everything a text item gets, and the mask only modulates how bright they are.
  * The mask carries its brightness as grey levels rather than as white with a
    varying alpha. MultiEffect reads the mask's colour, not its alpha, so a mask
    that is white everywhere multiplies by one everywhere and the band does
    nothing at all — which is exactly what the first attempt at the swap did.
  * Both inputs stay hidden. MultiEffect draws the items it is handed whether or
    not they are visible, but it does not hide them itself: a visible source
    draws the text twice, once plainly and once through the effect.

