lumina-lock (0.4.18) unstable; urgency=medium

  * The video wallpaper is hardware-decoded again on NVIDIA. Qt 6.8 loads its
    FFmpeg media backend unless told otherwise, and that backend sizes NVDEC's
    surface pool from the thread count — 16 threads on this box — while a
    3840x2400 h264 stream needs 39 surfaces, past the driver's limit of 32.
    cuvidCreateDecoder fails with CUDA_ERROR_INVALID_VALUE and Qt decodes in
    software from then on, silently: the only trace is an ffmpeg warning on
    stderr that nothing was reading. Measured on the stock 4K wallpaper, the
    decoder engine sat at 0% and playback burned 0.60 core. The GStreamer
    backend picks NVDEC instead (gst's nvh264dec outranks avdec_h264), so the
    lock now asks for it — but only when NVIDIA's driver is the one loaded, so
    VA-API and other vendors' hardware paths are left on the backend that
    handles them, and an explicit QT_MEDIA_BACKEND still wins.
  * Same wallpaper, same measurement, through the GStreamer backend: decoder
    engine 14-23%, 0.27 core. Frames arrive at VideoOutput.videoSink at the
    same 30 fps, so the reveal and poster logic did not need to change.

