lumina-lock (0.4.14) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Rebuilt the power menu around what each action costs, instead of around a
    list of eight rows that all weighed the same.
  * Shutting down is now a slide: pull the knob to the far end and let go. The
    screen dims as it travels, so the gesture shows its own price, and the
    label carries the update wording ("滑动以关机" / "滑动以更新并关机").
  * The other actions are buttons below it, each with an icon drawn as a path —
    no SVG plugin, no system icon theme to be installed, tinted by the theme and
    crisp at any size. Restart is the one that cannot be taken back, so it is
    the one that counts: holding it fills a ring around the disc, letting go
    cancels. Everything else acts on the press, because everything else can be
    undone.
  * Merged "update and restart" / "update and shutdown" into the two power
    actions. lastore runs the upgrade and then takes the machine down itself, so
    while updates are pending that is simply what restart and shut down mean —
    four entries about going away become two.
  * Fixed the protection being attached to the wrong actions. A single click on
    关机 armed it and then fired by itself 1.4 s later, with no second
    confirmation; and the armed row kept counting after the pointer moved
    elsewhere, so the action that ran was not the one on screen. Meanwhile
    更新并关机 (irreversible) had no protection at all and 注销 (recoverable) did.
  * Opening the menu no longer preselects 关机. Focus starts on the first button,
    which is 锁定 over the desktop and 待机 from the lock — a default selection is
    a guess at intent, and the only guess worth making is the cheapest action.
  * Actions the machine cannot perform are left out rather than greyed out: a
    disabled button advertises something that cannot happen.
  * The update wording now keys off lastore's UpgradableApps list rather than
    CheckUpdateMode. The latter is a policy setting — it reads 5 on this machine
    with nothing to install — so it said nothing about whether there was
    anything to do.
  * Fixed a state the menu could not come back from: when an action failed the
    menu stayed up with its press flag still set, leaving it dimmed and ignoring
    every key. There is now a fallback that lets the press go, the same way the
    window-hide has one.
  * Dropped the drifting gradient washes. Motion that carries no meaning is
    noise, and behind a slide the dim is the thing worth watching.

 -- Lumina <lumina@example.com>  Thu, 17 Sep 2026 22:20:00 +0800

lumina-lock (0.4.13) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Added the session power menu — what dde-lock provides as
    org.deepin.dde.ShutdownFront1. The dock's power button, the launcher's and
    any session-issued request reach it, and its .service file activates this
    same binary, so the process that owns the lock owns this too.
    The method and signal names are dde-lock's, so nothing that calls it can
    tell the difference; only the surface is new, and it is not a copy of the
    original's grid of tiles: a vertical stack of rows over a two-tone gradient
    that drifts while the menu is open, the selection carried by a marker that
    changes height and a chevron that eases in, the rows arriving one after
    another, and destructive rows that fill for a beat before acting — moving
    away or pressing Esc takes the arm back.
  * Availability is asked for rather than assumed: CanShutdown, CanReboot,
    CanLogout, CanSuspend and CanHibernate decide which rows are live, and the
    two update rows (UpdateAndShutdown / UpdateAndReboot, which hand the request
    to lastore's PrepareFullScreenUpgrade so the update daemon runs the upgrade
    and brings the machine down itself) are dimmed with a reason when there is
    no update daemon to hand them to.
  * Fixed a keyboard bug that this work exposed. ScreenManager reroutes a key
    press to whichever screen the pointer is on, and treated "no screen claimed
    yet" as another screen, so it consumed the key. With the power menu up, every
    key aimed at it was swallowed whenever the pointer sat anywhere but the auth
    screen — the normal case on a multi-screen desk. The menu now takes the
    keyboard for as long as it is up.
  * LUMINA_POWER_DRY_RUN=1 makes the menu log the action instead of performing
    it. It can only ever prevent an action, so it is harmless to ship.
  * Removed the jagged edges. Qt Quick draws rounded corners and scaled images
    without smoothing unless asked, and the panels that were already smooth
    (GlassPanel, and so the password pill) are the ones that had been asked:
    the avatar's circle and its mask, the scaled account picture, and the menu's
    rows, arm fill and marker were not. All of them now set antialiasing or
    smooth as appropriate.
  * Show the account's own picture instead of always drawing the initial. It
    comes from the same place dde-lock takes it: IconFile on the accounts service
    (system bus, /org/deepin/dde/Accounts1/User<uid>, interface
    org.deepin.dde.Accounts1.User), and it is used only when the file exists and
    is not empty — otherwise the initial stands, exactly as the reference does.
    Fetched asynchronously, because this is the lock's startup path.
    Two mistakes on the way there, both caught by running it rather than reading
    it: asking asyncCall for "org.freedesktop.DBus.Properties.Get" as a method on
    the *user* interface builds an invalid member name and libdbus aborts the
    process (the lock would have crashed on startup); and Properties.Get answers
    with a variant that Qt may already have unwrapped, so unwrapping
    unconditionally turned the path into nothing. The same unwrap bug was in the
    lastore CheckUpdateMode read, which would have handed the update daemon mode
    0.
  * Made the password character bigger (16 * unit -> 22 * unit) and put the
    border weight back to 1 * unit, which is where it started — an earlier
    "1.4 * unit" change never applied (the script that carried it aborted on its
    first edit), so the borders had stayed at 2 * unit throughout.
  * Removed the jagged edges where it is possible without new assets. Qt Quick
    leaves rounded corners and scaled images unsmoothed unless asked, and the
    panels that were already smooth (GlassPanel, and with it the password pill)
    are the ones that had been asked. The avatar's circle and mask, the scaled
    account picture, and the menu's rows, arm fill and marker now ask too.
    One edge remains stepped: the outside of a *picture* avatar, because Qt does
    not antialias a rounded Rectangle when it is rendered into a layer and a
    layer is what the mask has to be. Doing better needs either a mask image with
    the soft edge baked in, or a precompiled .qsb fragment shader (Qt 6 rejects
    inline GLSL — fragmentShader is a URL to a .qsb, and this project ships none).
  * Thickened the edges that are aimed at: the menu rows and the password pill
    now use a 2 * unit border instead of a hairline. Both scale with the screen
    like everything else, and the shutdown row's colour needs the weight to read
    as an edge rather than a tint.
  * Gave the menu an exit. It faded in and then cut: the scene gated its own
    visibility on "is it up", so on the frame the exit began the item vanished,
    and the window was hidden at the same instant. The scene now stays visible
    while it is leaving, and the window is hidden only once the exit has played —
    the scene reports back, with a 1.5s fallback because a window left up would
    hold the keyboard with nothing on screen. Measured across the exit, the
    screen goes 44 -> 41 -> 37 -> 29 -> 20 -> 13 -> 0: a decay, not a step.
  * Clicking past the rows dismisses the menu and returns to the desktop. The
    rows take their own clicks, so only the space around them is affected.
  * The shutdown row carries the danger colour on its border, selected or not:
    it is the one action that cannot be taken back.
  * Declared both surfaces' window flags in QML instead of setting them from C++
    after the window already existed. Setting a window flag makes Qt destroy and
    recreate the native window, so whether the window manager had already decided
    to manage it depended on how that recreation lined up with the mapping — the
    open animation appeared only some of the time. Declared up front there is
    nothing to race: xwininfo reports Override Redirect State: yes for the menu
    on its first showing and on every reopening, and for the lock window, at the
    screen's full size. showAll() now logs whether the flag is in place at the
    moment the window is mapped (bypassWM=true), which is the property that used
    to be timing-dependent.
  * Fixed the two update rows doing nothing. They addressed lastore under its
    classic name, com.deepin.lastore, which does not exist in the 6.x generation:
    the availability probe therefore answered "no update daemon" and both rows
    stayed dimmed, so clicking them could not do anything at all. They now use
    org.deepin.dde.Lastore1 / /org/deepin/dde/Lastore1 / ...Lastore1.Manager, and
    the probe reports updatesAvailable=true against the machine this was reported
    from. The show log carries that flag, so it can be confirmed at a glance.
  * Made the confirmation countdown restart rather than resume. Cancelling with
    Esc and arming the same row again continued from wherever the cancelled fill
    had stopped, because the fill's width was animated and left part-way: the
    second press could act almost immediately. A fresh arm now resets the
    progress to zero before counting. Measured: not fired 0.9s after re-arming,
    fired 1.4s after it.
  * Fixed the empty window left over the desktop after the first unlock — the
    lock's own long-standing bug, not something the menu introduced, and the
    reason the menu used to draw nothing when it borrowed those surfaces.
    Both the lock's window and the menu's declared `visibility: Window.FullScreen`
    in QML, which states what the window wants to be and fights the C++ that
    hides it: after an unlock the lock's window stayed mapped over the desktop
    (visible in the taskbar, clearable only with Alt+F4) and clicks never reached
    the desktop. Visibility is now owned by ScreenManager alone. Proven by the
    new logging, which reads the state *after* the hide: `hideAll after
    visible=false visibility=0`.
  * The menu's window is override-redirect on X11 — the same treatment the lock's
    own windows get — so the window manager neither animates it open nor lists
    it, and it is transparent, so the menu fades in out of the desktop rather
    than opening as a window.
  * The menu now draws in a window of its own instead of as a layer on the lock's
    surfaces. The lock's windows are hidden and re-shown around every lock and
    unlock, and this package's own logging showed the failure mode that follows
    from borrowing them: the menu reached a fully settled scene (shown, opacity
    1.0, correct size, eight rows) and nothing appeared, because the window it
    was drawing into had been left in a state the window manager would not
    present — after an unlock a surface stayed mapped over the desktop (visible
    in the taskbar, cleared only with Alt+F4). A window that only ever shows the
    menu has nothing else to go wrong, and the lock's window lifetime is no
    longer on the path at all.
    The window is sized from its screen the way the lock's windows are, because
    a window that is merely asked to go fullscreen keeps the geometry it was
    created with until the window manager answers — the first attempt drew the
    menu into a 160x160 corner.
  * Fixed two problems the first hands-on test of the menu turned up.
    - It blocked the GUI thread — and held the keyboard grab — while asking the
      session manager which actions were available. Those Can* calls are
      synchronous and ran inside a D-Bus method handler, so a session manager
      that was slow to answer, or that was itself waiting on our reply,
      deadlocked: the page never appeared and the session had no keyboard, which
      is indistinguishable from the lock having taken the machine hostage.
      Against a session manager that answers in 3s, Show() took 15.0s and is now
      measured at 0.01s. Availability is cached and refreshed asynchronously, and
      lastore's CheckUpdateMode is read asynchronously as well.
    - Shown over an unlocked session it flashed the lock's own clock before the
      menu: bringing the borrowed surfaces up starts the lock's entry reveal.
      Over an unlocked session the menu now covers the screen on its first frame
      and fades nothing; the rows keep their stagger, which happens over an
      already-opaque cover.

 -- Lumina <lumina@example.com>  Wed, 17 Sep 2026 15:10:00 +0800

lumina-lock (0.4.12) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Multi-screen: the authentication UI now follows the user instead of being
    pinned to the primary screen. Every screen runs the same surface, and
    exactly one of them is "active" at a time — it compacts the clock and
    reveals the password field, while the others stay in Idle (wallpaper,
    clock, hint) and can take the lock over at any moment.

    Which screen that is follows the interaction, like macOS: a click gives it
    to the screen that was clicked, and a key press gives it to the screen the
    pointer is on. The pointer is the only usable signal for the keyboard case,
    because the X11 grab delivers every keystroke to one window — the one
    holding the grab — which is not necessarily where the user is looking.
    ScreenManager therefore watches key presses application-wide: when a
    keystroke arrives for a screen other than the active one, the interactive UI
    moves there and the keystroke that woke it becomes the first character of
    that screen's password, instead of being typed into a field the user cannot
    see. The keyboard grab moves with the field, since the grab window is what
    receives the keys.

    The pointer is deliberately *not* grabbed any more. With one fullscreen
    window per screen the clicks already land on the lock, and an X11 pointer
    grab funnels every click into the grab window — which is exactly what would
    stop "click the screen you are looking at" from working. Alt+Tab / Super
    blocking is unaffected: that is the keyboard grab.

    This also removes a lock-out: unplugging or disabling the primary screen
    while locked used to destroy the only screen with a password field and
    release the input grab, leaving a lock with nothing to type into. The
    remaining screens can now always be woken, and when the active screen
    disappears the choice simply falls back.

  * SecondaryScreen.qml is gone: every screen runs LockScreen.qml, so a video
    wallpaper now decodes on every screen rather than only on the primary one
    (the decoder is still released while the lock is hidden). WallpaperHost
    drops its `playVideo` switch, which had no caller left.

  * Multi-screen smoke test in the README (Xvfb + xdotool), and the D-Bus
    smoke test still covers the lockFront surface.

 -- Lumina <lumina@example.com>  Wed, 16 Sep 2026 12:00:00 +0800

lumina-lock (0.4.11-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Restored the clock's entry growth, this time on purpose. Removing the
    accidental growth (the one that ran from zero) had also removed any size
    change from the entry, leaving only the fade. The type size is now a pure
    function of `unit` and two dimensionless progress values, `entryProgress`
    and `compactProgress`; only the progress values animate, and nothing
    animates a pixel size. A Behavior on the size can express neither: with the
    window sized after the first layout, an always-on Behavior animated the
    type out of nothing, and a Behavior gated by the reveal snapped instead,
    because the gate and the value it guards are released by the same signal —
    Qt assigns the new size before the gate opens. The clock now arrives at
    82% and settles over 560 ms, slightly longer than the 480 ms fade, so the
    growth is still in progress while the clock is visible. Measured: the
    glyph run grows about 10% across the visible part of the entry.

  * Fixed the wallpaper jumping from the flat cover colour to the video, and
    restructured how the wallpaper reveals itself. The cover used to sit
    underneath the video, so the video could only ever appear on top of it in
    a single frame. Every layer is now revealed by fading the layer above it
    out, never by fading content in: a Rectangle's and an Image's opacity
    reliably blend, a VideoOutput's does not. The cover is the last child,
    hides the whole stack until there is something to show, and dissolves away
    when there is. Verified against a bright moving test video: the transition
    is a bell-shaped cross-dissolve over ~11 frames, the signature of two
    different images blending, where a cut would be one frame at full
    difference with nothing either side.
  * The reveal also waits for the video's first frame to have been painted
    rather than merely delivered, and for the cover to finish dissolving before
    the clock is allowed to arrive, so the glass letterforms never sample a
    half-dissolved backdrop. Poster and cover now key off the sink's
    videoFrameChanged instead of the playback state, which is reached before
    anything is on screen.
  * Static images no longer fade in on their own; the cover's dissolve covers
    that case and the video case with one mechanism.

