lumina-lock (0.4.6-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Date animation no longer steps. QFont::setPixelSize takes an int, so
    animating font.pixelSize made the 22px date snap through four visible sizes
    across the transition while the 125px clock took forty-five and read as
    continuous — measured, the date's width moved in three 11px jumps where the
    clock's took seven finer ones. GlassText now animates a continuous `size`,
    rasterises the glyphs at the nearest integer size and applies a sub-pixel
    scale correction, so the motion interpolates smoothly and the glyphs stay
    crisp. Side effect: the date now renders at its designed 27*unit instead of
    being truncated to 22px, so it is ~1px larger in the idle state.
  * Entry no longer stutters. The full-screen blur was pre-warmed by drawing it
    for the first 12 rendered frames; because the blur chain runs even at amount
    0, that cost a full-screen multi-level blur per frame and the entry
    animation lost frames (a plateau mid-fade, measured). It is now warmed once,
    after the entry has settled, where the cost is invisible.
  * Settings page rewritten to the control-center conventions: every row carries
    backgroundType: DccObject.Normal, values moved into the row description, DTK
    flat combo boxes and a section title above the clock rows. Row heights,
    button sizes and paddings are left at the control center's defaults rather
    than overridden per item.
  * File pickers now open the *system* file dialog instead of the Qt Quick
    built-in one (DontUseNativeDialog is gone), matching every other
    control-center plugin.
  * The unlock hint fades and rises into place, then breathes.
  * A printable key that wakes the lock now becomes the first character of the
    password instead of being swallowed.
  * Password field: the dots are centred, each new character gives a short pop,
    a wrong password bounces (decaying sideways shake plus a springy dip rather
    than a flat slide) and a correct one flashes the accent colour and pulses
    outward before the scene fades.

 -- Lumina <lumina@example.com>  Tue, 15 Sep 2026 15:00:00 +0800

lumina-lock (0.4.5-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Fix the frozen start of the first Idle -> Authenticating transition in a
    lock process. The full-screen wallpaper blur was created with
    visible/blurEnabled off and switched on only when the transition begins, so
    its first render — compiling the level-3 blur shader and allocating the
    full-resolution multi-level FBO chain — landed inside the animation.
    Measured by recording the transition at 60 fps: 9 identical frames before,
    none after.
    The effect's blur items are now enabled from startup and it is drawn (at
    amount 0, which is a plain copy of the wallpaper) for the first frames the
    window renders, which is what actually compiles the shader. It is then
    hidden again: a video wallpaper dirties the scene every frame, and leaving
    the blur drawn permanently cost ~2.9 cores of software rasterisation on
    every idle frame (measured 483% vs 199% of one core).
    The warm-up counts rendered *frames*, not wall-clock time — the window's
    first paint can land later than any timer window would allow for, and a
    warm-up that ends before the first frame never draws the effect at all.
  * README: the transition description no longer claims the video is frozen
    during the exit (that was removed in 0.4.2).

 -- Lumina <lumina@example.com>  Mon, 14 Sep 2026 15:10:00 +0800

lumina-lock (0.4.4-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Pull the frosted glass edge softening back from ~4px to ~1.5px. 0.4.3
    removed the stair-stepping but at the cost of visibly out-of-focus type.
    Antialiasing at a fixed output resolution is a one-pixel ramp that no
    amount of supersampling can widen (rendering at 2x and scaling down just
    re-derives the same per-pixel coverage), so smoothing a large display
    glyph beyond its own coverage is inherently a blur. The chosen amount
    drops the steepest edge steps by ~20% while keeping the letterform crisp;
    GlassText.maskSoftness is the single knob.

 -- Lumina <lumina@example.com>  Mon, 14 Sep 2026 11:45:00 +0800

lumina-lock (0.4.3-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Frosted glass is now antialiased for real. The previous fix only shaped
    the mask's alpha ramp; the remaining stair steps came from the mask
    textures being consumed at 1:1, where a curve's edge can only ever be one
    pixel wide. The masks are now filtered (layer.smooth) and softened by a
    blur applied *inside* the mask layer.
    Note for future edits: a `layer.effect` on a mask item silently does
    nothing — Qt applies a layer effect while nesting the layer into the
    scene, and the mask item is never drawn — and the mask blur must use
    blurMax <= 16, because at blurMax 32 the blur runs at quarter resolution
    and the amount quantises into coarse steps (0.08 and 0.16 rendered
    identically).
  * Rounded shapes (the mask, the pill's fill and its 1px hairline) request
    antialiasing.
  * The password placeholder uses a stronger text colour, now that the pill
    itself is dimmed while authenticating.

 -- Lumina <lumina@example.com>  Mon, 14 Sep 2026 10:30:00 +0800

lumina-lock (0.4.2-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Antialias the frosted glass. The mask was sampled through a hard alpha
    threshold, which discarded the text renderer's antialiasing and left
    jagged glyph strokes and jagged rounded corners; the mask now uses a
    smooth alpha ramp.
  * The clock's font-size transition animates again. The type is now driven by
    scalar font properties instead of an alias to the child Text's grouped
    `font`, which a Behavior cannot target — that alias turned the Idle →
    Authenticating transition into a hard switch. All scene transitions also
    share one duration and easing, so the clock, the authentication panel and
    the background dimming move as a single gesture.
  * The password field no longer stays bright while the rest of the scene dims
    during authentication: the glass mirrors the scene dimming, which it does
    not inherit because it samples the wallpaper rather than the composed
    scene.
  * Unlocking no longer pauses a video wallpaper — playback continues through
    the exit fade.

